#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192657 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192588 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "990e3660-dfd7-50fa-b60b-0478ff02a073" :question/math-academy-id "e-14002" :question/problem "Find the solution $(u,v)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5u-6v=3 \\\\ -3u+2v=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $v$-coefficient in the first equation is $-6,$ while in the second equation it is $2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $3,$ then the $v$-coefficient will become $6,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $-6$ in the first equation.\n![](images/e7/e701d299976539abdbbe6e9c311c2db14c23cca6817c5a3de5b5c05a95760d99.svg)\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $v$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}5u-\\cancel{6v} & =3 \\\\ -9u+\\cancel{6v} & =-15 \\\\ (5u-9u) & =(3-15) \\\\ -4u & =-12 \\\\ u & =3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $u = 3.$ However, we still need to find $v.$ To do this, we substitute $u = 3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3u+2v & =-5 \\\\ -3(3)+2v & =-5 \\\\ -9+2v & =-5 \\\\ 2v & =4 \\\\ v & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u = 3, \\,v = 2,$ which can be expressed as $(3,2).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509345 :question-generator/id #uuid "24c9299c-e1f2-5131-9fa7-77c01dfe4bb1" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186192657.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d19e5318-830b-5397-b5d5-db4b2ea4edd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191159} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d7c2ec-2218-5685-bd6d-a27b265e6cce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191160} {:answer/id #uuid "41d06511-304d-52ec-8660-cebfa1226e4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191161} {:answer/id #uuid "68ebd117-4cb9-5c3f-911c-a45f8215fa93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191162} {:answer/id #uuid "73de3fc5-e6cf-5c66-bccb-fd58d2b8db1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191163}] :answer-field/id #uuid "84dcebbe-0ca8-598a-800e-4c91899feb7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191158}] :question/id #uuid "ac5edd25-6b22-5574-95a5-6e2d817dd860" :question/math-academy-id "q-170145" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n${3x - 2y =-1; 4x + y =-5$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5deaa590-2cc0-5e91-b357-5a9ea981fe20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5deaa590-2cc0-5e91-b357-5a9ea981fe20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508529} :answer-field/id #uuid "24fdb15c-a8b3-5277-abc3-5f3c2cfa726d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191552} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6e8f613-08b6-5743-b480-77cc2a7e2ff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6e8f613-08b6-5743-b480-77cc2a7e2ff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508699} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a2a4186-c9b8-58ca-9163-304a07832933" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "659bfd82-a0b2-5112-81eb-2185b857af31" :question/math-academy-id "q-238087" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x+2y=11 \\\\ 7x+4y=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x,y)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $y.$\n\nThe solution is $($\n{{field-1}}$\\,,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $2,$ while in the second equation it is $4.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $-2,$ then the $y$-coefficient will become $-4,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,-5x+2y=11\\,)\\,\\times (-2) \\\\ 7x+4y=5\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}10x-4y=-22 \\\\ 7x+4y=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x-\\cancel{4y} & =-22 \\\\ 7x+\\cancel{4y} & =5 \\\\ (10x+7x) & =(-22+5) \\\\ 17x & =-17 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=-1.$ However, we still need to find $y.$ To do this, we substitute $x=-1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x+4y & =5 \\\\ 7(-1)+4y & =5 \\\\ -7+4y & =5 \\\\ 4y & =12 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-1,\\,y=3,$ which can be expressed as $(-1,3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508927 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60ff1fa4-ffc7-5336-b3af-c476f0ceefb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508536} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3b6243-75d7-5af0-9491-17de5aeb8a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508553} {:answer/id #uuid "7e050490-5650-55a2-bbaf-c33b2adf2dd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508573} {:answer/id #uuid "c386a187-6f74-5af7-a28a-842f181e5f36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508690} {:answer/id #uuid "f8157de3-2831-5e1e-a4bb-0158173f239a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60ff1fa4-ffc7-5336-b3af-c476f0ceefb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508536} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1429b72-180d-5d2a-ae39-57cc2cf7b25c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c472f9d-cfda-5414-a840-ee6d18544bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-170159" :question/problem "Find the solution $(p,q)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3p+2q=11 \\\\ 5p+4q=19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $q.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $q$-coefficient in the first equation is $2,$ while in the second equation it is $4.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $-2,$ then the $q$-coefficient will become $-4,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,3p+2q=11\\,)\\,\\times (-2) \\\\ 5p+4q=19\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-6p-4q=-22 \\\\ 5p+4q=19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $q$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}-6p-\\cancel{4q} & =-22 \\\\ 5p+\\cancel{4q} & =19 \\\\ (-6p+5p) & =(-22+19) \\\\ -p & =-3 \\\\ p & =3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $p=3.$ However, we still need to find $q.$ To do this, we substitute $p=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p+2q & =11 \\\\ 3(3)+2q & =11 \\\\ 9+2q & =11 \\\\ 2q & =2 \\\\ q & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=3,\\,q=1,$ which can be expressed as $(3,1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91e9c308-67f3-5e17-a945-9f7ca8b964e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91e9c308-67f3-5e17-a945-9f7ca8b964e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509311} :answer-field/id #uuid "147b96a0-420d-536f-930a-bf1ab6057f76" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509266} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fee1e55-5f1d-5256-8a99-8662fe215f83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fee1e55-5f1d-5256-8a99-8662fe215f83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509268} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7e7709b-4144-5bdd-89e3-266ed5cdd337" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15551398-1d65-5cfc-9388-3441da458ef3" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+y&=16\\\\-7x-6y&=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $y$.\n\nThe solution is $($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $1$, while in the second equation it is $-6$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $6$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-6$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ \\left(5x+y=16\\right)\\cdot (6); -7x-6y=-4\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n30x+6y&=96 \\\\\n-7x-6y&=-4 \\\\\n(30x-7x)&=(96-4) \\\\\n23x&=92 \\\\\nx&=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 4$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x+y&=16 \\\\\n5(4)+y&=16 \\\\\n20+y&=16 \\\\\ny&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 4, \\,y = -4\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(4,-4)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "200edcc6-55df-50a4-aa4a-486132c110af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{28}{11},- \\frac{339}{22})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509269} {:answer/id #uuid "59eca543-875a-5f9c-906c-2227556ef87f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509292} {:answer/id #uuid "9bae6aad-69de-515b-ae98-f68c8a8cd5c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{15}{11},- \\frac{124}{11})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509315} {:answer/id #uuid "c8204af7-b37e-5cf3-8cb6-a7dd64791ca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509329} {:answer/id #uuid "fb2229f3-7217-5ef8-ada8-8b297feb7340" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8204af7-b37e-5cf3-8cb6-a7dd64791ca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509329} :answer-field/id #uuid "f87fcfbe-ec51-5267-986b-e924e05d49d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21904fad-7e59-5888-9a6b-ef099e3648bc" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+6y&=-17\\\\-7x+2y&=-13\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $6$, while in the second equation it is $2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-3$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $6$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ x+6y=-17; \\left(-7x+2y=-13\\right)\\cdot (-3)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx+6y&=-17 \\\\\n21x-6y&=39 \\\\\n(x+21x)&=(-17+39) \\\\\n22x&=22 \\\\\nx&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 1$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-7x+2y&=-13 \\\\\n-7(1)+2y&=-13 \\\\\n-7+2y&=-13 \\\\\n2y&=-6 \\\\\ny&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 1, \\,y = -3\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(1,-3)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509271 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f908001-8c0e-5ad5-bf2c-a4aae8e1a691" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f908001-8c0e-5ad5-bf2c-a4aae8e1a691" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509264} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc24fa13-ffeb-5ff7-9dbb-b1d479e2964b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509330} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e86f70f-2c51-5c48-9d22-0d5dc6889c88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e86f70f-2c51-5c48-9d22-0d5dc6889c88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509263} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5fd3803-15e2-50c5-94bb-4bdef29b8a99" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "23b70eba-8c0c-5171-b139-0ac2370d20a6" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+14y&=-36\\\\-7x-2y&=-20\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $y$.\n\nThe solution is $($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $14$, while in the second equation it is $-2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $7$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-14$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $14$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ 5x+14y=-36; \\left(-7x-2y=-20\\right)\\cdot (7)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x+14y&=-36 \\\\\n-49x-14y&=-140 \\\\\n(5x-49x)&=(-36-140) \\\\\n-44x&=-176 \\\\\nx&=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 4$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-7x-2y&=-20 \\\\\n-7(4)-2y&=-20 \\\\\n-28-2y&=-20 \\\\\n-2y&=8 \\\\\ny&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 4, \\,y = -4\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(4,-4)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509278 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b382fd6-c1bc-58a5-ab3b-203bfcd1d3f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509275} {:answer/id #uuid "35155a6e-f543-56cc-895d-88462084d35c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509277} {:answer/id #uuid "8ee68739-5943-5dba-a7b3-6a408d7f228a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509309} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7dc056-edde-5f96-a671-5ccf85ff5755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{64}{5},- \\frac{99}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509324} {:answer/id #uuid "f8dcd307-0ccd-537f-902c-16a48c2e11dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35155a6e-f543-56cc-895d-88462084d35c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509277} :answer-field/id #uuid "76d0f030-a9e9-5940-9a6d-793769aabcc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3775b5ec-0d42-5ee6-ba36-8700f462d97a" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+y&=-7\\\\x-6y&=22\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $1$, while in the second equation it is $-6$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $6$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-6$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ \\left(-x+y=-7\\right)\\cdot (6); x-6y=22\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6x+6y&=-42 \\\\\nx-6y&=22 \\\\\n(-6x+x)&=(-42+22) \\\\\n-5x&=-20 \\\\\nx&=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 4$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x+y&=-7 \\\\\n-1(4)+y&=-7 \\\\\n-4+y&=-7 \\\\\ny&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 4, \\,y = -3\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(4,-3)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26cfe277-3842-5747-9ee0-6c4247fead55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509273} {:answer/id #uuid "450b54a2-ea5c-555a-b4d6-fe9b7a919372" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,27)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509283} {:answer/id #uuid "97dffbe1-1bb5-5124-8577-4fb6de014f06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{23}{13},\\frac{264}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509313} {:answer/id #uuid "a2934f92-f656-5bd6-8747-1b6db11bc275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509316} {:answer/id #uuid "f434a981-e2a2-536d-8559-c0efa231bcb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{68}{13},\\frac{399}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26cfe277-3842-5747-9ee0-6c4247fead55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509273} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b38b033-45ad-5b3e-b165-1a07d9c9cdc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "575d3d25-ee48-560a-beb0-60eb637f5795" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+4y&=8\\\\3x+y&=15\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $4$, while in the second equation it is $1$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-4$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-4$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ -x+4y=8; \\left(3x+y=15\\right)\\cdot (-4)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x+4y&=8 \\\\\n-12x-4y&=-60 \\\\\n(-x-12x)&=(8-60) \\\\\n-13x&=-52 \\\\\nx&=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 4$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x+y&=15 \\\\\n3(4)+y&=15 \\\\\n12+y&=15 \\\\\ny&=3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 4, \\,y = 3\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(4,3)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509302 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13e27713-0c32-5160-bf48-8794b4ef56ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509265} {:answer/id #uuid "37d9b91c-a905-57ce-81a0-85f4eccb157f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{1}{2},- \\frac{11}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509279} {:answer/id #uuid "82de173e-ffef-54ef-bc13-55db11bad3d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,- \\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509306} {:answer/id #uuid "c1fbed6e-fb34-5ad3-8ef9-483b59c4fa39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509325} {:answer/id #uuid "e6a705a0-e0c3-5f06-aaae-681a63958822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1fbed6e-fb34-5ad3-8ef9-483b59c4fa39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509325} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7e82569-1864-5896-a52b-68af45977480" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78207f23-12ee-53c5-9f78-9395f0921474" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y&=5\\\\7x-6y&=11\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $-2$, while in the second equation it is $-6$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $-3$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-6$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ \\left(x-2y=5\\right)\\cdot (-3); 7x-6y=11\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x+6y&=-15 \\\\\n7x-6y&=11 \\\\\n(-3x+7x)&=(-15+11) \\\\\n4x&=-4 \\\\\nx&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = -1$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = -1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx-2y&=5 \\\\\n1(-1)-2y&=5 \\\\\n-1-2y&=5 \\\\\n-2y&=6 \\\\\ny&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = -1, \\,y = -3\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(-1,-3)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30dff6f0-44a2-569f-9cf5-16833973d9d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{48}{43},- \\frac{895}{86})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509276} {:answer/id #uuid "5445dbb7-2643-5c0f-a09e-cd8ccf6fd047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509286} {:answer/id #uuid "6ba4ae51-6c73-5240-afd2-12a95ee94548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509297} {:answer/id #uuid "77ffeb13-0d3e-595c-929a-d62967cf28a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509301} {:answer/id #uuid "cd313717-399a-59fe-9882-55714ce7fdb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{113}{43},- \\frac{675}{43})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77ffeb13-0d3e-595c-929a-d62967cf28a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509301} :answer-field/id #uuid "b210daf5-f7c9-544f-a57c-87c7b7a3a074" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "888444ab-20f1-5ac7-85e7-9089399fd1bb" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+12y&=-35\\\\-7x+2y&=-13\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $12$, while in the second equation it is $2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-6$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-12$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $12$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ x+12y=-35; \\left(-7x+2y=-13\\right)\\cdot (-6)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx+12y&=-35 \\\\\n42x-12y&=78 \\\\\n(x+42x)&=(-35+78) \\\\\n43x&=43 \\\\\nx&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 1$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-7x+2y&=-13 \\\\\n-7(1)+2y&=-13 \\\\\n-7+2y&=-13 \\\\\n2y&=-6 \\\\\ny&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 1, \\,y = -3\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(1,-3)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c580255-e760-5f33-8c6a-2550a573a799" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-22,52)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509262} {:answer/id #uuid "57ef6484-bebb-5270-9134-2a94851e1343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509289} {:answer/id #uuid "65e68218-6593-5454-a9fc-d17eedb52630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{10}{11},- \\frac{8}{11})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509295} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b0f169-c30c-552b-b0d7-efdfc48e9c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509335} {:answer/id #uuid "eadf7382-2d55-5dc8-a450-064d2a4cf6fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57ef6484-bebb-5270-9134-2a94851e1343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509289} :answer-field/id #uuid "2658620e-2fcd-55d0-9ddc-b610c83e058d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "90d0118c-789c-54fc-9fd4-a48e58b92b30" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x-2y&=6\\\\3x+10y&=14\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $-2$, while in the second equation it is $10$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $5$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-10$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $10$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ \\left(-5x-2y=6\\right)\\cdot (5); 3x+10y=14\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-25x-10y&=30 \\\\\n3x+10y&=14 \\\\\n(-25x+3x)&=(30+14) \\\\\n-22x&=44 \\\\\nx&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = -2$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = -2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x-2y&=6 \\\\\n-5(-2)-2y&=6 \\\\\n10-2y&=6 \\\\\n-2y&=-4 \\\\\ny&=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = -2, \\,y = 2\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(-2,2)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d2421c4-3e95-5a88-8c94-267fc39c0f35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509298} {:answer/id #uuid "7135885d-50ad-52a2-b1d8-046d4c8b8464" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509299} {:answer/id #uuid "7935b5f6-9317-5d13-a182-f177d4cf51e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509303} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac1beda-ed4c-5a56-9a2c-7d43cc5905f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509304} {:answer/id #uuid "b920b780-bd99-5c37-9e3d-f93a43c3c601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ac1beda-ed4c-5a56-9a2c-7d43cc5905f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509304} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3a96850-9a06-5e2c-8ce5-899e594c0915" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad8b1c30-588c-535a-9132-16c79af9dfe2" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+4y&=-1\\\\x+2y&=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $4$, while in the second equation it is $2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-2$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-4$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ 5x+4y=-1; \\left(x+2y=1\\right)\\cdot (-2)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x+4y&=-1 \\\\\n-2x-4y&=-2 \\\\\n(5x-2x)&=(-1-2) \\\\\n3x&=-3 \\\\\nx&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = -1$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = -1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx+2y&=1 \\\\\n1(-1)+2y&=1 \\\\\n-1+2y&=1 \\\\\n2y&=2 \\\\\ny&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = -1, \\,y = 1\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(-1,1)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66300ce6-b475-5d0f-b089-ac8eec2807a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66300ce6-b475-5d0f-b089-ac8eec2807a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509296} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fec54cb-9c85-5252-8582-cea05545c3ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509285} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5709927f-d51f-5749-a92a-072b5998a2f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5709927f-d51f-5749-a92a-072b5998a2f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509287} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3de5a92-faa1-5773-9a97-9f5b9fa3c657" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4b00c48-3459-54dd-a97b-4752b54d2e62" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+8y&=-12\\\\-7x-2y&=-20\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $y$.\n\nThe solution is $($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $8$, while in the second equation it is $-2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $4$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-8$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $8$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ 5x+8y=-12; \\left(-7x-2y=-20\\right)\\cdot (4)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x+8y&=-12 \\\\\n-28x-8y&=-80 \\\\\n(5x-28x)&=(-12-80) \\\\\n-23x&=-92 \\\\\nx&=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 4$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-7x-2y&=-20 \\\\\n-7(4)-2y&=-20 \\\\\n-28-2y&=-20 \\\\\n-2y&=8 \\\\\ny&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 4, \\,y = -4\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(4,-4)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b4363e1-914f-5143-818c-f9a250cf4675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{9}{19},- \\frac{265}{57})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509280} {:answer/id #uuid "58d587da-c716-5846-9cf4-4ad93c3d7829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509291} {:answer/id #uuid "93e6532a-ea86-5e9b-b388-cca7bcfcdbde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509312} {:answer/id #uuid "b17056e7-740c-572d-a1a8-6081d649e85d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{95}{3},\\frac{151}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509319} {:answer/id #uuid "d05f6b87-d487-5741-a054-888fc5a7888a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,- \\frac{23}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93e6532a-ea86-5e9b-b388-cca7bcfcdbde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509312} :answer-field/id #uuid "07e1e4bf-ace0-570f-9bd6-71262f095a75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d5c3f388-7843-5364-910c-9e97bb540c6d" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x-3y&=13\\\\5x-21y&=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $-3$, while in the second equation it is $-21$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $-7$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $21$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-21$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ \\left(-2x-3y=13\\right)\\cdot (-7); 5x-21y=-4\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n14x+21y&=-91 \\\\\n5x-21y&=-4 \\\\\n(14x+5x)&=(-91-4) \\\\\n19x&=-95 \\\\\nx&=-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = -5$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = -5$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x-3y&=13 \\\\\n-2(-5)-3y&=13 \\\\\n10-3y&=13 \\\\\n-3y&=3 \\\\\ny&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = -5, \\,y = -1\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(-5,-1)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ecbbb5e-3ed2-528e-830c-64e987e989da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509281} {:answer/id #uuid "42e7ebb4-26b8-5fd9-b9bf-6edb7cf93ed3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509282} {:answer/id #uuid "843072f9-f614-5e55-8bf0-12fd95bdb012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509307} {:answer/id #uuid "991361f4-364a-57db-97a8-04a5f7037455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509314} {:answer/id #uuid "ad644440-f651-5dad-b438-815e63a116ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,- \\frac{29}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42e7ebb4-26b8-5fd9-b9bf-6edb7cf93ed3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509282} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bccdb9c-e6cc-546c-bc1f-af909a73f4f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef7f3552-0ad8-5545-8673-2b2894d40ad4" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+6y&=-33\\\\2x-3y&=21\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $6$, while in the second equation it is $-3$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $2$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $6$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ -x+6y=-33; \\left(2x-3y=21\\right)\\cdot (2)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x+6y&=-33 \\\\\n4x-6y&=42 \\\\\n(-x+4x)&=(-33+42) \\\\\n3x&=9 \\\\\nx&=3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 3$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x-3y&=21 \\\\\n2(3)-3y&=21 \\\\\n6-3y&=21 \\\\\n-3y&=15 \\\\\ny&=-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 3, \\,y = -5\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(3,-5)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509344 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "238f847f-5009-5183-b9bd-b8e136f0ba4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28d9b9f6-af1e-5d04-81f6-d3e14d15ba37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{33}{5},- \\frac{166}{15})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509274} {:answer/id #uuid "4dee1735-757a-5a7e-8abc-3d08ac98a01a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- \\frac{19}{33},\\frac{562}{99})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509284} {:answer/id #uuid "581c1cfe-6f4b-5f91-ad28-ceabe0e592f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509290} {:answer/id #uuid "61a29bde-d4a9-528b-90d5-e13febae164b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509294} {:answer/id #uuid "b681415e-0e9c-5a64-970d-72037254e2cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{20}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "581c1cfe-6f4b-5f91-ad28-ceabe0e592f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509290} :answer-field/id #uuid "8275be3d-f06e-52aa-9799-9ef432738ad0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fe868340-7a35-5fed-a08d-ce25cbc1fbb9" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+15y&=32\\\\-7x-3y&=-13\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $15$, while in the second equation it is $-3$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $5$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-15$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $15$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ 2x+15y=32; \\left(-7x-3y=-13\\right)\\cdot (5)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x+15y&=32 \\\\\n-35x-15y&=-65 \\\\\n(2x-35x)&=(32-65) \\\\\n-33x&=-33 \\\\\nx&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 1$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-7x-3y&=-13 \\\\\n-7(1)-3y&=-13 \\\\\n-7-3y&=-13 \\\\\n-3y&=-6 \\\\\ny&=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 1, \\,y = 2\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(1,2)$."}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"}]}
